\section{Choosing the clock}{

The clock shape is chosen when the model is constructed, through the
\code{clock} argument, and is fixed for the whole fit:
\itemize{
\item \code{RDMSWTN_UT()} or \code{RDMSWTN_UT(clock = "linear")} is the linear clock,
the default. The gain \code{1 + u*x} rises by \code{u} per second of decision
time and doubles after \code{1/u} seconds. It shortens slow tails moderately.
\item \code{RDMSWTN_UT(clock = "exponential")} is the exponential clock. The gain
\code{exp(u*x)} doubles every \code{log(2)/u} seconds, so slow tails are cut much
harder.
}

Both shapes use the same parameter \code{u} (inverse seconds, sampled on the
log scale, default 0), so a design written for one runs unchanged under
the other. The same \code{u} means different things, though: at \code{u = 1} the
linear gain after 2 s is 3 while the exponential gain is \code{exp(2)}, about
7.4. Estimates of \code{u} are therefore not comparable across clocks. To
compare the shapes, fit each model separately and compare the fits (for
example by information criteria). The shape cannot vary by condition
within one model; the rate \code{u} can, through the design.

The clock is per accumulator row, so the design may let \code{u} vary by
accumulator. One gain per trial, shared by all accumulators (\code{u ~ 1}, or
a condition such as \code{u ~ Load}), is the natural default: only a shared
clock leaves choice probabilities unchanged. With \code{u} omitted from the
formula it stays at its default of 0, and the model is exactly
\code{\link[=RDMSWTN]{RDMSWTN()}}.
}

